[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-111172"] :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-135332/selection/2aef90071200a7bc65baa2d03f49803233ca7b2dc16865265136281ee914878b" :answer/id #uuid "0c6fc6de-7dfc-5357-8fda-45910592362f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135332/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "4040e585-d06d-5d2c-b4cc-d0968af049a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135332/selection/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" :answer/id #uuid "f9da4089-4c8b-5d47-962e-cf5e8c656f76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135332/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "b1992c48-f7a6-57df-955b-18a805990c31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135332/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "7acc9f03-b514-553b-8784-e2c889d11c09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135332/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f63035f-5aea-5270-9863-f3dfdee74117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135332/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer-field/choices ["q-135332/selection/2aef90071200a7bc65baa2d03f49803233ca7b2dc16865265136281ee914878b" "q-135332/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" "q-135332/selection/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" "q-135332/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" "q-135332/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135332" :question/id #uuid "c3f9b59b-88c0-5727-bd50-b0bfab146b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-135332" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{5}{8}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-135332/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135332"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-135352/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "2bd79e96-93b6-5775-9ce5-56ef8b44d102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135352/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "8eaed15e-46e6-5333-a758-12d2ceaf8ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135352/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "43ba3beb-6fac-5b4a-b678-27adc4ac2340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135352/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "cab41cb3-d341-5338-a715-44e774bdc44c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135352/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "728f7df7-07c1-5a12-936a-231f3aeef33d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135352/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "707ee803-2e7d-5f5e-8585-e37060062d6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135352/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-135352/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-135352/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-135352/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-135352/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-135352/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135352" :question/id #uuid "079e403e-14d4-565f-89cb-9d848b0de7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-135352" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{14}{10}{x}_{3}=\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2(\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{20}{5}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-135352/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135352"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-135409/selection/1a9977ab0c4e7f2926aa5591c2181eef1547cd08d0edb113288bdfe08f050dfe" :answer/id #uuid "9e1736e0-1621-5f23-88fa-2d4cfec1a8cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "85\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135409/selection/a22ab1ef026d025376c1576ea3fa79feea443f69eda6dc935f5972fc0cb2f208" :answer/id #uuid "78faf2dc-7b80-549e-99a6-c192020b7780" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135409/selection/aef8669f54d004acc9e3a6b0d942a949a71408430406851d11769f9b3fe5f442" :answer/id #uuid "0d1eccd1-1a22-505b-90ab-f6e5b4cbfe87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135409/selection/727469a25af95313f02f443e7904e4cfb04fd1797af64ed2bbb8ce56efae1953" :answer/id #uuid "fd4f12e7-2d81-55f6-a8f4-0261c521d2cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135409/selection/e2475d6a0d5fcc1630a2b114e8098ab669b74d2574dee2dadcff3ddf3e017eae" :answer/id #uuid "77a29e3d-34ef-5f8e-a811-6414240a63ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135409/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "50858a1c-12bf-5fa3-a8ba-822f1e78454a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135409/selection/a22ab1ef026d025376c1576ea3fa79feea443f69eda6dc935f5972fc0cb2f208" :answer-field/choices ["q-135409/selection/1a9977ab0c4e7f2926aa5591c2181eef1547cd08d0edb113288bdfe08f050dfe" "q-135409/selection/a22ab1ef026d025376c1576ea3fa79feea443f69eda6dc935f5972fc0cb2f208" "q-135409/selection/aef8669f54d004acc9e3a6b0d942a949a71408430406851d11769f9b3fe5f442" "q-135409/selection/727469a25af95313f02f443e7904e4cfb04fd1797af64ed2bbb8ce56efae1953" "q-135409/selection/e2475d6a0d5fcc1630a2b114e8098ab669b74d2574dee2dadcff3ddf3e017eae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135409" :question/id #uuid "0ca4a36a-56ac-5467-b1dd-a66f29862b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135409" :question/problem "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually consume extra-refined sugar?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.84 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.16 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the customers will eventually use extra-refined sugar. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this type of sugar is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 450\\,000=75\\,000.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-135409/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135409"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-135433/selection/b8d923aba2346b816103492243af8202151f47759dacc14a295e634d5ca3d959" :answer/id #uuid "7b941b3a-23bb-5841-89b9-759c7bf35f90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.2$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135433/selection/8141c25d9d20a58cfb90e03cb789fb996f9692e58866a4f9c04b971edcaa2d1a" :answer/id #uuid "149ec104-b0c4-5e52-9a79-27cff048f48a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135433/selection/5e5de02ceb99f79328e4dac74bcbab83c408d6800f2f8691b0d71d718baf527b" :answer/id #uuid "ee0677b4-7f71-51ee-b1c1-e260ea65735f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.9$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135433/selection/d21baf340bb2650d1c29dec143ef5cfe4481c277564bf5fcb8a1cd87fa306d83" :answer/id #uuid "c9a8916e-4741-518b-b650-b4659b1cafd6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.6$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135433/selection/783a92ec06a702e5ca88f6c358d6fc75c6328545d5640aee0f7da9fa464dea24" :answer/id #uuid "d75df1c5-4cdb-595a-b1de-4dcba024fb8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.8$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135433/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb12a8b1-9b6a-5b63-9685-15a4f54a9da7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135433/selection/783a92ec06a702e5ca88f6c358d6fc75c6328545d5640aee0f7da9fa464dea24" :answer-field/choices ["q-135433/selection/b8d923aba2346b816103492243af8202151f47759dacc14a295e634d5ca3d959" "q-135433/selection/8141c25d9d20a58cfb90e03cb789fb996f9692e58866a4f9c04b971edcaa2d1a" "q-135433/selection/5e5de02ceb99f79328e4dac74bcbab83c408d6800f2f8691b0d71d718baf527b" "q-135433/selection/d21baf340bb2650d1c29dec143ef5cfe4481c277564bf5fcb8a1cd87fa306d83" "q-135433/selection/783a92ec06a702e5ca88f6c358d6fc75c6328545d5640aee0f7da9fa464dea24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135433" :question/id #uuid "b4cd14dc-370c-5165-b4c6-4dfaf5fbe2b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-135433" :question/problem "A study has recorded and analyzed the results of the parliamentary elections of a particular country, held every four years. The study shows how voter preference changes from one political party to another. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Yellow Party** | **Blue Party** | **Red Party** |\n| To: | **Yellow Party** | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Blue Party** | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $0.3$ |  |\n| **Red Party** | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of the people who voted for the yellow party in the last election will vote for the red party in the next election.\n\nIf the electorate contains $5.6$ million voters and the given probabilities remain constant election after election, how many people will vote for the red party after many elections have taken place?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many elections, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 7{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{28} \\\\ \\frac{15}{28} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many elections, $\\frac{1}{7}$ of the people will vote for the red party. Therefore, the number of people that will vote for the red party equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 5.6\\,\\text{million}=0.8\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-135433/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135433"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
